  <main id="main-content" class="guide-main" style="--accent:#0868D9;--accent-soft:#EFF6FF;--accent-border:#BFDBFE;">
    <div class="container guide-layout">
      <article class="guide-card">
        <div class="guide-label">PipelineSync field notes</div>
        <h2>The practical breakdown</h2>
        <p class="intro">A pipeline can look healthy in a dashboard while the team experiences something very different: deals sit in the wrong stages, close dates drift, and forecasts become a negotiation. The fix is not another report. It is a shared definition of progress that reflects how buyers actually move.</p>
        <section><h3>1. Make every stage observable</h3><p>A stage should describe a verifiable customer milestone, not an internal task. “Proposal sent” is an activity; “commercial terms confirmed” is progress. Give each stage a clear entry condition, exit condition, and owner so two reps would classify the same deal the same way.</p><ul><li>Write a one-sentence exit criterion for every stage.</li><li>Remove stages that exist only to represent rep activity.</li><li>Add required fields only when they improve the decision at that stage.</li></ul></section><section><h3>2. Separate pipeline hygiene from forecasting</h3><p>Forecasting is a decision about expected revenue. Pipeline hygiene is the quality of the records supporting that decision. Track both. A deal with no next step, an overdue close date, or no recent activity should be visible as a data-quality risk, not quietly included in the forecast.</p><ul><li>Create a next-step and next-step-date standard.</li><li>Use close-date movement as a coaching signal.</li><li>Report on stalled deals and stage aging by owner.</li></ul></section><section><h3>3. Make the operating rhythm consistent</h3><p>Even a well-designed pipeline fails when the team uses it differently every week. Pair the CRM rules with a short weekly inspection: what changed, what is stuck, what needs help, and which records need correction?</p><ul><li>Review stage aging and close-date changes weekly.</li><li>Coach from the same definitions used in reporting.</li><li>Keep the pipeline model stable long enough to learn from it.</li></ul></section>
        <section>
          <h3>What to change this week</h3>
          <p>Use this short checklist to turn the article into an operating improvement.</p>
          <div class="guide-checklist"><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Stage exit criteria published</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Next-step standard enforced</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Stalled deals visible</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Close-date movement reviewed</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Forecast and hygiene separated</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Weekly inspection scheduled</div></div></div>
        </section>
        <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> If your pipeline does not reflect reality, start by fixing the definition of progress. Better reporting follows better operating discipline.</p></div>
